  <h1>What is a Wiki?</h1>
  
  <p>A wiki is a collaborative website or knowledge base that anyone (with proper permissions) can edit directly from the web browser. The word "wiki" comes from the Hawaiian word for "quick," reflecting how easy it is to create and modify content.</p>

  <h2>Key Characteristics of Wikis</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Collaborative:</strong> Multiple people can contribute to and improve content together</li>
    <li><strong>Easy to Edit:</strong> Simple formatting and no need for technical knowledge</li>
    <li><strong>Version Control:</strong> Changes are tracked and can be reverted if needed</li>
    <li><strong>Interconnected:</strong> Pages can link to each other, creating a web of knowledge</li>
    <li><strong>Publicly Accessible:</strong> Information is available to anyone who needs it</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common Uses for Wikis</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Project documentation and knowledge bases</li>
    <li>Team handbooks and procedures</li>
    <li>Company culture and best practices</li>
    <li>Educational resources and learning materials</li>
    <li>Community-driven encyclopedias</li>
    <li>Product and software documentation</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Welcome to Wiki Farm</h2>
  <p>Wiki Farm is your space to create, grow, and share knowledge. Start by creating your first wiki or exploring wikis created by others. Whether you're documenting a project, building a knowledge base, or collaborating with your team, Wiki Farm makes it simple and enjoyable.</p>

  <p style="margin-top: 2rem; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-muted);">Ready to get started? <a href="/">Head back home</a> to explore or create your first wiki.</p>
</div>

